  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA (descriptive, verb-disciplined) ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>Iran's parliamentary National Security and Foreign Policy Commission approved Article 3 of the Strategic Action Plan for Security and Development of the Strait of Hormuz on 2026-08-23, establishing a fee regime for maritime services (safety, environmental, fuel, insurance) for permitted-country vessels transiting the strait, with payment accepted in rial or other designated currency (IRNA; Mehr News; TRT World; Al Jazeera live blog 2026-08-23). One day earlier, IRNA reported that Iran granted permission for a number of Iraqi oil tankers to transit Hormuz following repeated appeals from Baghdad delivered during Parliament Speaker Ghalibaf's visit. → moved <span class="cite">F2</span>, <span class="cite">F4</span></p>

      <p>US Treasury Secretary Bessent used a 2026-08-23 statement to promise an economic D-Day ahead of the expected new Iran sanctions rollout, while Iranian security chief Mohsen Rezaei told Press TV that Tehran would neutralize the economic war and warned Tehran would halt oil flow through Hormuz if neighboring states join the US crackdown (CNN live 2026-08-22 and 08-23; France 24 2026-08-22). Iran's foreign ministry called Trump's newly announced sanctions framework an assertion of extraterritorial sovereignty over every independent Member State of the United Nations (Al Jazeera 2026-08-22). Trump on 2026-08-18 told reporters no talks with Iran are underway. → moved <span class="cite">F3</span>, <span class="cite">F6</span></p>

      <p>Israeli warplanes conducted overnight airstrikes on 2026-08-21 around Nabatieh — targeting Kfar Rumman and the Ali al-Taher Heights — with additional artillery fire across southern Lebanon, per Lebanese media aggregated by Middle East Monitor and Times of Israel. This followed the 2026-08-15 France 24-reported strikes that killed 11 in southern Lebanon and continued a pattern of near-daily kinetic activity despite the stalled June framework agreement covering Lebanon-Israel de-escalation (US-brokered, envisaging phased IDF withdrawal, LAF deployment, and Hezbollah disarmament). → moved <span class="cite">F1</span></p>

      <p>Hormuz commercial throughput remained at crisis-floor levels: Lloyd's List Intelligence brief 2026-08-19 recorded 73 transits across Aug 10-16 (down from 91 the prior week, roughly 10/day 7-day average), with UANI's 2026-08-17 shipping update noting one spot day at a single vessel against the pre-crisis 73/day baseline. Iran-Oman route negotiations reported by CBS and Al Jazeera 2026-08-21 continued on certain technical points per FM spokesman Baghaei, with framework awaiting final approval on the Iranian side. → supports <span class="cite">F4</span> without move</p>

      <p>Israeli domestic politics: Likud's 2026-08-17 primary elevated Cohen, Ohana, Levin and Regev to the top of the party slate at low turnout (Haaretz 2026-08-17 and 08-18, Times of Israel primary coverage), while Yashar under Eisenkot continues to lead in most Aug 19-20 polls at 24-26 seats vs Likud's 22-23. Given F5's Sunday-only cadence, this is tracked but not scored intra-week. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

