        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>IRNA and Mehr News carried near-identical framings of the parliamentary Hormuz service-fees committee vote, presenting Article 3 as a lawful assertion of Iranian sovereignty over maritime services in a strategic waterway and quoting Baghaei's statement that when Iran provides maritime services it must be reimbursed for them. IRNA also emphasized that the Iraqi tanker transit permissions were granted at Baghdad's request during Speaker Ghalibaf's visit, casting Iran as arbitrator of regional oil access rather than blockader. → moved <span class="cite">F2</span></p>

      <p>Press TV's 2026-08-22 and 08-23 coverage centered on Rezaei's warning to halt Hormuz oil flow if Gulf neighbors accede to the US economic war, and on Deputy FM Gharibabadi's earlier characterization of the Islamabad MOU as being in a coma from which revival requires Washington changing its approach. Iranian officials continued to deny active bilateral talks with the US while framing Bessent's D-Day language as evidence that Washington, not Tehran, is responsible for the diplomatic freeze. → moved <span class="cite">F3</span>, <span class="cite">F6</span></p>

      <p>Kayhan and Fars-adjacent commentary (via GlobalSecurity translations for the 2026-08-17 IRNA piece) reiterated the coma framing and criticized US secondary sanctions posture toward China, describing Beijing as a strategic partner whose economic ties to Iran will not yield to Treasury pressure. State-media output remained defiant but produced no announcement of imminent kinetic action from Iranian territory. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

